\section{Preparing a wheat-genome BAM from ONT reads}{


To determine where non-specific PCR products originate, map the demultiplexed
ONT reads against a complete wheat reference genome. For example, the Chinese
Spring bread-wheat assembly IWGSC CS RefSeq v2.1 is available from NCBI as
assembly \code{GCF_018294505.1}. Assume the downloaded and decompressed FASTA is:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode text">}}\preformatted{GCF_018294505.1_IWGSC_CS_RefSeq_v2.1_genomic.fna
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

and the sample reads are:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode text">}}\preformatted{barcode150.fastq.gz
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

First build a reusable minimap2 index. This is done once for the wheat
reference and can require substantial memory and disk space because the
assembly is approximately 14 Gb:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode bash">}}\preformatted{minimap2 -d wheat_CS_RefSeq_v2.1.mmi \\
  GCF_018294505.1_IWGSC_CS_RefSeq_v2.1_genomic.fna
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

Minimap2 normally indexes a limited number of target bases in one batch
(\code{-I}, commonly 8G by default). The wheat assembly is larger than one default
batch, so its \code{.mmi} can be a multi-part index. When mapping against such an
index, provide \code{--split-prefix} so minimap2 has a writable prefix for its
temporary intermediate files and can combine the index parts correctly.

Align the ONT reads with the \code{map-ont} preset, convert the SAM stream to a
coordinate-sorted BAM, and create its index:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode bash">}}\preformatted{minimap2 -ax map-ont -t 32 \\
  --split-prefix=barcode150.wheat.minimap2_split \\
  -R '@RG\\tID:barcode150\\tSM:barcode150\\tPL:ONT' \\
  wheat_CS_RefSeq_v2.1.mmi barcode150.fastq.gz \\
| samtools sort -@ 8 -m 2G -o barcode150.wheat.bam -

samtools index -@ 8 barcode150.wheat.bam
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

\verb{-t 32} controls minimap2 threads, while \verb{samtools sort -@ 8 -m 2G} uses up
to 2 GB per sorting thread. Adjust both settings to the available computer.
\code{--split-prefix} is a filename prefix, not an output BAM name. Put it on a
fast local disk with sufficient free space when possible, for example
\verb{/scratch/project/barcode150.minimap2_split}. It must be writable and must be
unique for every simultaneously running sample; reusing one prefix in
parallel jobs can corrupt or overwrite temporary files. Interrupted jobs may
leave files with this prefix, which can be removed after confirming that no
minimap2 process is still using them.

The \code{-R} read-group field is optional for this function but records the sample
identity in the BAM. \verb{samtools sort} is essential before indexing; the final
\code{-} tells it to read SAM/BAM data from the pipe.

On a machine with enough RAM, an alternative is to rebuild a single-part
wheat index by setting \code{-I} above the reference size, for example:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode bash">}}\preformatted{minimap2 -I 20G -d wheat_CS_RefSeq_v2.1.single.mmi \\
  GCF_018294505.1_IWGSC_CS_RefSeq_v2.1_genomic.fna
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

This can require substantially more memory. For typical servers, retaining
the multi-part index and using a unique \code{--split-prefix} is safer.

Do not apply a high MAPQ filter while creating the archival BAM unless disk
space requires it. Bread wheat is hexaploid and contains many highly similar
A-, B-, and D-subgenome loci. Genuine reads from homologous or repetitive
regions can therefore receive MAPQ 0 or have secondary alignments. Retaining
these records allows comparison of \code{min_mapq = 0}, \code{20}, or \code{21} later. With
\code{primary_only = TRUE}, the function reports only the primary placement of
each alignment record; use \code{primary_only = FALSE} when investigating all
alternative placements.

Validate and summarize the BAM before analysis:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode bash">}}\preformatted{samtools quickcheck -v barcode150.wheat.bam
samtools flagstat barcode150.wheat.bam
samtools idxstats barcode150.wheat.bam \\
  | sort -k3,3nr \\
  | head -30
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

\code{quickcheck} prints nothing when the BAM passes its basic checks. \code{flagstat}
reports mapped, secondary, supplementary, duplicate, and other flag counts.
In \code{idxstats}, the third column is the number of mapped alignment records for
each reference sequence. NCBI FASTA files commonly use accessions such as
\code{NC_...} as BAM reference names. Use those exact names in \code{reference_name};
they can be inspected with \verb{samtools idxstats} or in
\code{result$reference_summary}.

If only a smaller diagnostic BAM is desired, mapped primary records with
strict \code{MAPQ > 20} can be written as follows:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode bash">}}\preformatted{samtools view -b -F 2308 -q 21 barcode150.wheat.bam \\
| samtools sort -@ 8 -o barcode150.wheat.primary.mapq21.bam -
samtools index barcode150.wheat.primary.mapq21.bam
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

Here \verb{-F 2308} excludes unmapped (\code{0x4}), secondary (\code{0x100}), and
supplementary (\code{0x800}) records, and \verb{-q 21} means MAPQ at least 21. This
smaller file cannot later be used to inspect the excluded placements, so the
unfiltered \code{barcode150.wheat.bam} should normally be retained.
